**Given:**

dfrac{64}{49}⁻dfrac{³{2}} times dfrac{2}{7}³ div sqrt[4]{(16)⁻³}

**Formula used:**

a⁻ⁿ = dfrac{1}{a^n}

(a^m)^n = am times ⁿ

sqrt[n]{a} = adfrac{¹{n}}

**Calculation:**

Solving each term separately,

dfrac{64}{49}⁻dfrac{³{2}} = dfrac{1}{(dfrac{64}{49})dfrac{³{2}}} = dfrac{1}{dfrac{8³}{7³}} = dfrac{7³}{8³} = dfrac{343}{512}

dfrac{2}{7}³ = dfrac{8}{343}

sqrt[4]{(16)⁻³} = (16)⁻³/⁴ = dfrac{1}{16³/⁴} = dfrac{1}{(2⁴)³/⁴} = dfrac{1}{2³} = dfrac{1}{8}

Putting all the values in the expression:

dfrac{64}{49}⁻dfrac{³{2}} times dfrac{2}{7}³ div sqrt[4]{(16)⁻³}

⇒ dfrac{343}{512} times dfrac{8}{343} div dfrac{1}{8}

⇒ dfrac{343}{512} times dfrac{8}{343} times 8

⇒ dfrac{343 times 8 times 8}{512 times 343} = dfrac{64}{512} = dfrac{1}{8}

**∴ The correct answer is option (1).**
